window.SOURCE_PAGES=[{"source":"angles","page":1,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes Week 2: Lift platforms angles and fundamental aerodynamics The starting point. Week 1 introduced the aircraft’s axes and motion. The next question is how that motion sets the angles at which the wing and tail meet the air, and how those angles produce aerodynamic loads. The question for this lecture: if the aircraft’s attitude, flight path and control settings are known, what lift will its wing and tail produce? attitude and path wing incidence wing lift and wake tail angle and load Follow the sequence from aircraft geometry to local airflow and aerodynamic loading. The tail sits in air already disturbed by the wing. The route through the lecture 1. Use aircraft attitude, flight path and wing setting to find chord incidence, then locate its zero-lift reference. 2. Recap aerofoil geometry, lift, drag and the effect of camber. 3. Extend the section model to a finite wing and its wake. 4. Construct tail incidence and add elevator and tab effects. Today the aim is to predict the loads produced by prescribed angles and controls. Week 3 will use force and moment balance to find the loads needed for equilibrium. 1"},{"source":"angles","page":2,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes Three questions guide the lecture This lecture develops three questions: 1. What angle does the wing meet the air at? Use aircraft attitude, flight path and wing setting, then locate the zero-lift reference. 2. How does that angle produce aerodynamic loading? Use the aerofoil response and account for the finite wing’s induced flow. 3. What happens at the tail? Find its local incidence, include the controls, and distinguish tail force from elevator hinge torque. Working assumptions. A rigid, symmetric aircraft in predominantly low-speed flow. Linear lift laws apply in the attached-flow range. 2"},{"source":"angles","page":3,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes The surfaces and their controls nose plan view ailerons elevator vertical tail: side view fixed fin rudder hinge nose rudder moves sideways to change yaw Surface Control Principal rotational effect Wing ailerons roll Horizontal tail elevator pitch Vertical tail rudder yaw The elevator changes the horizontal tail’s aerodynamic loading. A small tab on the elevator also changes its hinge moment. This week develops the wing and horizontal tail. Lateral-directional behaviour returns in Week 6. 3"},{"source":"angles","page":4,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 1 / AIRCRAFT ANGLES Separate aircraft attitude from flight path fuselage datum horizontal V air : flight path θ γ wing section fuselage airflow past the aircraft Side view with a fuselage and a wing section. The datum is fixed in the fuselage; V air is the aircraft velocity relative to the air. θ pitch attitude: fuselage datum above the horizontal γ flight-path angle: air-relative velocity above the horizontal The fuselage datum is θ − γ above the forward air-relative velocity direction. The airflow past the aircraft travels in the opposite direction to V air . Using forward-pointing reference directions allows comparison of the small incidence angles. Can the nose be above the horizon while the aircraft descends? Identify the signs of θ and γ. The fuselage is now located relative to the air. Next, include how the wing is mounted on that fuselage. 4"},{"source":"angles","page":5,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 1 / AIRCRAFT ANGLES Add the wing setting to find chord incidence The wing chord joins the leading and trailing edges. Its fixed angle above the fuselage datum is the chord setting i g . forward velocity reference fuselage datum direction wing chord θ − γ i g α g T.E. L.E. airflow The view is rotated so that the forward air-relative velocity reference is horizontal; the true horizon is omitted. The datum direction is translated parallel to itself for the angle construction. Chord incidence α g : the angle from the forward air-relative velocity direction to the chord. Start at the flow reference, turn through θ − γ to reach the fuselage datum, then through i g to reach the chord: α g = (θ − γ) + i g = θ + i g − γ. Read the signs physically: pitching up increases α g ; increasing the wing setting increases it; tilting the flight path upwards at fixed attitude decreases it. With the chord fixed in space, move the velocity direction closer to the chord. What happens to α g ? This is a geometric angle. It can be calculated before choosing an aerodynamic lift model. 5"},{"source":"angles","page":6,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 1 / AIRCRAFT ANGLES Apply the geometry to level, climbing and descending paths Use the chord-based relation α g = θ + i g − γ in three prescribed flight geometries. V level θ = 10 ◦ , γ = 0 ◦ V climb θ = 18 ◦ , γ = 10 ◦ V descent θ = 8 ◦ , γ = −8 ◦ In each case the chord setting is i g = 2 ◦ . The schematic aircraft and path arrows show the signs; these are independent geometric examples. Case θ γ α g = θ + i g − γ Level path 10 ◦ 0 ◦ 12 ◦ Climbing path 18 ◦ 10 ◦ 10 ◦ Descending path 8 ◦ −8 ◦ 18 ◦ Which case has the largest chord incidence? Why is it not the case with the highest nose attitude? Attitude alone does not determine incidence. Whether a given incidence produces attached flow or stall depends on the wing’s aerodynamic response. 6"},{"source":"angles","page":7,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 1 / AIRCRAFT ANGLES Find the aerofoil’s zero-lift reference The chord incidence is known. Does a chord aligned with the flow also mean zero lift? For a cambered aerofoil, generally it does not. 1. Rotate the section until its lift is zero chord line no-lift line parallel to airflow −α 0 zero lift α = 0, α g = α 0 < 0 airflow 2. Rotate the same section until the chord is parallel to the airflow chord line no-lift line −α 0 positive lift T.E. L.E. α g = 0, α = −α 0 > 0 airflow The same cambered section is rotated in a fixed airflow. The unsigned gap between chord and no-lift directions is −α 0 = |α 0 | for α 0 < 0. Zero-lift angle α 0 : the chord incidence at zero lift; it is negative for this positively cambered example. No-lift line: a reference parallel to the airflow at zero lift. It rotates with the section; its angle to the chord is set by the zero-lift condition. α = α g − α 0 . α g = 0 =⇒ α = −α 0 > 0. Model scope. In first-order thin-aerofoil theory, α 0 follows from the camber line; thickness does not contribute at that order. The reference stays fixed for a chosen configuration and flow condition in the approximately linear, attached-flow range. Real data can change with Reynolds number and separation. Zero lift is not zero loading. Surface pressure, drag and a pitching moment can remain when the net lift is zero. 7"},{"source":"angles","page":8,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 1 / AIRCRAFT ANGLES Connect chord incidence to the zero-lift reference The flight geometry has already given α g . Camber adds a change of reference; it does not change the aircraft’s orientation. motion and mounting θ, γ, i g chord incidence α g incidence above zero lift: α geometry subtract α 0 One forward calculation: first locate the chord relative to the air, then measure the same orientation from the section’s zero-lift reference. α g = θ + i g − γ, α = α g − α 0 = θ + i g − γ − α 0 . For later wing–tail calculations it is convenient to define the matching no-lift-line setting: i w = i g − α 0 =⇒ α = θ + i w − γ . Both versions give the same body-to-path angle: α g − i g = α − i w = θ − γ. Scope of the wing model. The simple untwisted wing model uses one representative chord and zero- lift reference. A twisted wing has spanwise-varying section references; a whole-wing zero-lift angle must come from the wing’s integrated lift response. Choose either (α g , i g ) or (α, i w ) consistently. 8"},{"source":"angles","page":9,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 1 / AIRCRAFT ANGLES Flight-test check: what incidence does the wing see? During a flight test, an aircraft is making a shallow climb. At the instant being checked, its pitch attitude is θ = 2 ◦ and its air-relative flight path is γ = 1 ◦ above horizontal. The wing chord is mounted i g = 3 ◦ above the fuselage datum. Aerodynamic data give a zero-lift angle α 0 = −1.2 ◦ for the simple wing model. Question. What wing incidence should be used to predict lift: first relative to the chord, then relative to zero lift? horizontal V: flight path fuselage datum wing chord γ = 1 ◦ θ = 2 ◦ i g = 3 ◦ α g =? Reference directions translated to one origin. Angular gaps are enlarged so they can be marked during the lecture; the labels give the flight-test values. chord above horizontal: θ + i g = 2 + 3 = 5 ◦ , chord above flight path: α g = 5 − 1 = 4 ◦ , incidence above zero lift: α = 4 − (−1.2) = 5.2 ◦ . The fuselage is 2 ◦ nose-up, but the wing meets the air at 4 ◦ to its chord. Its lift law measured from zero lift uses 5.2 ◦ . If the flight-path angle becomes 4 ◦ with attitude and setting unchanged, what are α g and α? The incidence needed for an aerodynamic calculation is now known. Next, establish how the aerofoil turns that incidence into lift and drag. 9"},{"source":"angles","page":10,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 2 / AEROFOIL AERODYNAMICS - RECAP Aerofoil aerodynamics: chord, thickness and camber Aircraft motion and wing setting determine incidence. The next aim is to relate that incidence to lift and drag. Shape and incidence set the pressure distribution over the section; adding those surface forces gives its aerodynamic load. Symmetric aerofoil T.E. L.E. chord length c t max (schematic) mean camber line coincides with chord Cambered aerofoil T.E. L.E. mean camber line straight chord line airflow • Chord: the straight line joining the leading and trailing edges; its length is c. • Mean camber line: the line midway between the upper and lower surfaces. Its displacement from the chord describes camber. • Thickness: separation of the upper and lower surfaces; t max /c is the maximum thickness-to-chord ratio. For an ideal symmetric section, the chord is also the no-lift line. For a cambered section, the two reference directions generally differ. Thickness and camber are different shape properties: a section can have finite thickness and zero camber. 10"},{"source":"angles","page":11,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 2 / AEROFOIL AERODYNAMICS - RECAP From an aerofoil section to lift and drag coefficients An aerofoil is a two-dimensional section model. Its lift L ′ and drag D ′ are forces per unit span; a complete wing produces total forces L W and D W . section: side view L ′ (N/m) D ′ c airflow whole wing: plan view S W span b strip dy L W = Z b/2 −b/2 L ′ (y) dy The section view shows the force directions. Adding the lift of the spanwise strips gives whole-wing lift: a load per metre becomes a total force. The factor 1 2 ρV 2 is the dynamic pressure. Aerofoil section Whole wing Lift c l = L ′ 1 2 ρV 2 c C W L = L W 1 2 ρV 2 S W Drag c d = D ′ 1 2 ρV 2 c C W D = D W 1 2 ρV 2 S W Load units L ′ , D ′ in N/m L W , D W in N Lower-case c l , c d describe a section; upper-case C W L , C W D describe the complete wing. V is the undis- turbed speed. Training-aircraft example: Diamond DA20-C1. Published wing area S W = 11.6 m 2 and maximum mass 800 kg. For an illustrative condition, take V = 40 m/s, ρ = 1.225 kg/m 3 and C W L = 0.70: 1 2 ρV 2 = 980 Pa, L W = 980(11.6)(0.70) = 7.96 kN. This is close to the aircraft’s 7.85 kN weight at that mass: a realistic force scale for a light trainer. A hypothetical 1 m chord with c l = 0.70 instead gives L ′ = 686 N/m, a local load per metre of span. Aircraft data: EASA TCDS IM.A.223 (area); Diamond Aircraft training brochure (mass). Speed and lift coef- ficients are teaching assumptions. 11"},{"source":"angles","page":12,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 2 / AEROFOIL AERODYNAMICS - RECAP A symmetric aerofoil: lift and drag behave differently For an ideal symmetric section in symmetric flow, lift is zero when the chord is parallel to the airflow. The chord and no-lift line coincide, so α 0 = 0 and α = α g . α g c l stall: lift reaches a peak 0 lift rises approximately linearly before the stall region α g c d 0 nonzero drag at zero lift drag is already present at zero lift and rises strongly near stall Qualitative section curves, shown from zero towards positive incidence. The vertical scales differ and no stall angle is prescribed. The dashed line is the attached-flow lift approximation. c l ≃ a ∞ α in the approximately linear range. Lift: the coefficient is nearly proportional to incidence until separation invalidates the straight-line model. The curve can depart from linearity before stall; the marked stall peak is the maximum lift coefficient in this sketch, not zero lift. Drag: skin friction and the streamwise pressure force produce profile drag. Drag remains nonzero at zero lift and generally rises strongly as separation develops. If α = 0 and c l = 0, must c d also be zero? Locate that condition on both graphs. The section drag shown here is profile drag. The extra induced drag caused by finite span enters later. 12"},{"source":"angles","page":13,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 2 / AEROFOIL AERODYNAMICS - RECAP A cambered aerofoil can lift at zero chord incidence For the positively cambered section shown below, the lift curve crosses zero at a negative chord inci- dence α 0 . The section therefore produces positive lift when its chord is parallel to the free stream. α g (deg) c l stall region ∆α g ∆c l α 0 linear model applies here Schematic cambered-section response. The straight part supports a linear model; extending it through the stall region does not. c l ≃ a ∞ (α g − α 0 ) = a ∞ α, α = α g − α 0 . Two reference lines describe the same section orientation: α g uses the chord, and α uses the no-lift line. At α g = 0: c l = −a ∞ α 0 > 0 if α 0 < 0. At α g = α 0 : c l = 0. The lift law uses the angular distance from the zero-lift crossing to the operating point. 13"},{"source":"angles","page":14,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 2 / AEROFOIL AERODYNAMICS - RECAP Thin aerofoil theory: choose a slope and its units The lift-curve slope gives the change in section lift coefficient for a small change in incidence: ∆c l = a ∞ ∆α. Thin aerofoil theory. For a thin two-dimensional section in incompressible, attached flow at small incidence, neglecting viscous effects, a ∞ = 2π 1/rad ≃ 6.283 1/rad. The ideal theory predicts the linear slope; it does not predict stall. Camber shifts the zero-lift angle in this first-order model. Thickness-based slope estimate (Hoerner, Lift): a ∞ ≃ 5.7 1 + 0.8 t max c 1/rad. For t max /c = 0.12, this gives a ∞ ≃ 6.2472 1/rad. This is an alternative estimate of the section slope. a ∞ = 2π 1/rad ∆α = π/180 rad a ∞ = 0.1097 1/deg ∆α = 1 deg both give ∆c l = 0.1097 The same one-degree incidence increase, using a slope per radian or per degree. Unit conversion: a deg = a rad π/180. Use radians with a slope in 1/rad. Check: with a ∞ = 2π 1/rad, a 2 ◦ increase gives ∆c l = (2π)(2π/180) = 0.2193. 14"},{"source":"angles","page":15,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 2 / AEROFOIL AERODYNAMICS - RECAP A flap changes camber and shifts the lift curve A downward flap changes the section’s effective camber. At the same chord incidence, it generally increases lift in the attached-flow range. neutral flap flap trailing edge down α g c l symmetric cambered flap down dots: stall peaks α 0 0 linear response −→ stall and separation Qualitative curves on the same chord-incidence axis. Dots mark illustrative stall peaks; their angles and heights are not prescribed data. The nearly parallel portions compare the linear response only. • Positive camber and flap deflection move the zero-lift crossing towards more negative incidence in this example. • For the same lift coefficient in the linear range, less chord incidence is required. On approach, flaps also allow the required lift to be produced at a lower airspeed. • The straight-line approximation fails before the stall peak. Flap deflection also changes maximum lift and stall behaviour. The control changes the lift response, but it does not remove the stall limit. 15"},{"source":"angles","page":16,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 2 / AEROFOIL AERODYNAMICS - RECAP Worked example: read a cambered aerofoil’s lift curve Given: a ∞ = 5.7 1/rad, α 0 = −2 ◦ , chord incidence α g = 5 ◦ and a stated stall angle of 12 ◦ . Find: incidence above zero lift and the section lift coefficient, using the supplied linear model. α g (deg) c l (5 ◦ , 0.6964) −2 5 α = 7 ◦ above zero lift The operating point is seven degrees above the zero-lift crossing. This is the given linear model; the graph does not predict the stalled response. α = α g − α 0 = 5 − (−2) = 7 ◦ = 0.122173 rad, c l = a ∞ α = 5.7(0.122173) = 0.6964. The chord incidence is below the supplied stall angle. The calculation uses the example’s stated linear model; being below stall does not make every real lift curve exactly straight. Using 5 ◦ directly would give c l = 0.4974 and miss the camber contribution. First measure the incidence from the zero-lift condition, then convert it to radians. The section model is now established. A finite wing alters the local flow, so its lift slope will differ from the section slope. 16"},{"source":"angles","page":17,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 3 / FROM SECTION TO WING Finite span changes the airflow An aerofoil section model has no wing tips. A real wing has a finite span, so air can move around its tips under the pressure difference between the lower and upper surfaces. 1. Pressure difference and flow around the tips fuselage upper surface: p < p ∞ lower surface: p > p ∞ wing 2. Trailing vortices induce downward motion downwash between the vortices clockwise anticlockwise Rear-view schematics for positive lift. The upper drawing locates the pressure difference and fuselage; the lower drawing shows the trailing-vortex rotation and downward induced motion. 1. The pressure difference drives flow around the tips. 2. The wing’s trailing vortex system induces a downward component of velocity at the wing. 3. The changed local-flow direction reduces effective incidence and tilts the aerodynamic force rear- wards. Compared at the same free-stream incidence: a smaller lift response and an additional induced-drag component. The next steps are to draw the incidence reduction, derive the wing lift slope, and resolve the tilted force. 17"},{"source":"angles","page":18,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 3 / FROM SECTION TO WING The local section sees a smaller incidence free-stream reference wing no-lift reference local-flow reference actual local airflow α i α eff α representative symmetric section actual velocity components V w i local airflow α i The local airflow travels backwards and downwards, so its forward reference tilts upwards towards the wing line. α eff = α − α i , α i ≃ w i V . V is the undisturbed speed; w i is the magnitude of the downward velocity induced at the wing. The velocity triangle gives tan α i = w i /V; at small angles, α i ≃ w i /V in radians. The wing geometry enters through aspect ratio: AR = b 2 S W , b = span, S W = wing area. For a rectangular wing this reduces to AR = b/c. A glider’s long, slender wing is a familiar high- aspect-ratio example. Ideal elliptically loaded, unswept wing: α i = C W L πAR . Lift creates an induced angle, and that angle reduces the incidence producing lift. Both effects must be solved together. 18"},{"source":"angles","page":19,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 3 / FROM SECTION TO WING Derive the finite-wing lift slope Model: unswept wing of sufficiently high aspect ratio, attached linear flow and ideal elliptic loading. a ∞ is the section lift slope; a is the complete-wing slope. α is measured from zero lift using the undisturbed flow, and α eff uses the local flow. 1. Apply the section response to the ideal wing: C W L = a ∞ α eff . 2. Substitute the induced-angle correction: C W L = a ∞   α − C W L πAR ! . 3. Collect the terms containing lift coefficient: C W L 1 + a ∞ πAR = a ∞ α. C W L = aα, a = a ∞ 1 + a ∞ /(πAR) . AR a (1/rad) a ∞ = 2π 4 8 12 20 2 4 6 For a ∞ = 2π 1/rad, increasing aspect ratio brings the wing slope closer to the section slope. Why must a < a ∞ in this model? What happens as AR becomes very large? 19"},{"source":"angles","page":20,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 3 / FROM SECTION TO WING Worked example: recover the effective angle Given: a ∞ = 2π 1/rad, AR = 8, α = 5 ◦ ; use the ideal model on page 19. a = 2π 1 + 2/8 = 5.0265 1/rad, C W L = 5.0265 5 π 180 = 0.4386, α i = 0.4386 π(8) = 0.01745 rad = 1.00 ◦ , α eff = 5.00 ◦ − 1.00 ◦ = 4.00 ◦ . free-stream reference wing no-lift reference local-flow reference actual local airflow α i α eff α representative symmetric section actual velocity components V w i local airflow α i Read the answer back into the geometry: five degrees supplied, one degree lost to induced flow, four degrees effective. Check: a ∞ α eff = 2π(4π/180) = 0.4386. Both routes give the same lift coefficient. 20"},{"source":"angles","page":21,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 3 / FROM SECTION TO WING Induced drag is a component of a tilted force L D i R α i local airflow travels backwards and downwards V The force normal to the local flow has an upward lift component and a rearward induced-drag component. Profile drag is omitted. L = R cos α i , D i = R sin α i . D i L = tan α i ≃ α i , D i ≃ Lα i . The approximation uses radians. One local-flow change Two consequences Smaller effective incidence α eff = α − α i Rearward force component D i /L ≃ α i At fixed lift, what happens to induced drag if the small induced angle doubles? Week 3 combines drag contributions in an aircraft polar. The next section follows the wing’s disturbed flow downstream to the tail. 21"},{"source":"angles","page":22,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 4 / THE TAILPLANE The tail flies in the wing’s disturbed flow The wing changes the flow reaching the tail. Read the airflow arrows from left to right in this figure; the aircraft’s nose is on the left. Source: J. B. Russell, Performance and Stability of Aircraft (1996). Read the lines first. AC is the straight extension of the undisturbed approach-flow direction; BD is the local flow at the tail. AE and CE are the wing and tailplane no-lift lines. Angle What it compares α Wing no-lift line AE with the undisturbed flow direction AC. ε Undisturbed direction BC with local tail flow BD: the downward turning of the flow. α T Tailplane no-lift line CD with local flow BD. η T Tailplane and wing no-lift lines, CE and AE: the fixed tail setting. The figure’s n.l.l. means no-lift line; its α T is written α T in the equations below. V e labels the undis- turbed approach flow; V denotes its speed in the equations. Downwash changes the flow direction at the tail. With aircraft attitude and tail setting fixed, more downwash reduces tail incidence. The angle ε at the tail need not equal α i at the wing. Here the local tail speed is approximated by V, so its dynamic pressure is 1 2 ρV 2 . 22"},{"source":"angles","page":23,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 4 / THE TAILPLANE A downwash gradient describes a response For the fixed configuration used in these examples, approximate the downwash at the tail by a straight line through the origin: ε = k ε α, k ε = dε dα . α (deg) ε (deg) ε = 0.4α ∆α ∆ε 0 5 10 The gradient measures the extra downwash produced by an incidence increment. This graph uses k ε = 0.4. ∆α = 1 ◦ =⇒ ∆ε = 0.4 ◦ . Example: α = 6 ◦ gives ε = 2.4 ◦ . Both angles may be in degrees or both in radians; their ratio is the same. The input α uses the wing’s no-lift reference. A different configuration or reference may require an offset; the through-origin model is the stated approximation for this lecture. More wing incidence also means more downwash at the tail. The tail therefore receives only part of the incidence increase. 23"},{"source":"angles","page":24,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 4 / THE TAILPLANE Use the two triangles to find tail incidence Use the construction on page 22. The two triangles ACE and BCD have the same angle at C, because A, B, C lie on one straight line and C, D, E lie on another. A B C D E α ε α T η T same angle at C The straight construction lines from the aircraft figure, with the body omitted. B lies on AC and D lies on CE; the dashed segment BD is the local-flow direction. triangle ACE : α + η T + ∠C = 180 ◦ , triangle BCD : ε + α T + ∠C = 180 ◦ . The common angle cancels, so the other two angles have the same sum: α T + ε = α + η T =⇒ α T = α + η T − ε. The illustrated tail setting is positive. In calculations, keep η T signed: positive means that the tail no-lift direction is nose-up relative to the wing’s. The equation also applies to the negative settings used in these examples. Hold α and η T fixed. If the flow turns farther downwards, why must α T decrease? 24"},{"source":"angles","page":25,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 4 / THE TAILPLANE The tail sees part of an incidence increment Substitute the zero-offset downwash model into the geometry: α T = (1 − k ε )α + η T , dα T dα = 1 − k ε . α (deg) α T (deg) no downwash k ε = 0.4 −3 5 10 0.6∆α For η T = −3 ◦ , the downwash gradient k ε = 0.4 reduces the response slope from 1 to 0.6. The setting determines the intercept. ∆α = 1 ◦ =⇒ ∆α T = 0.6 ◦ . Does changing the fixed tail setting change the slope of these straight lines? Setting shifts the line; the downwash gradient changes its slope. This distinction will matter in static stability. 25"},{"source":"angles","page":26,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 4 / THE TAILPLANE Elevator and tab angles use their own references The fixed tailplane, hinged elevator and smaller tab have separate roles. The dashed outlines below mark their neutral positions. fixed tailplane tail no-lift reference elevator hinge elevator tab η L T α T local airflow Tab detail: reference moves with elevator neutral tab deflected tab β tab elevator tab hinge Side view rotated to make the tail no-lift reference horizontal; leading edge to the right. Elevator angle η is measured from neutral; tab angle β tab is relative to the elevator. Both are positive with their trailing edges down. α T tail no-lift reference relative to local flow η elevator deflection from neutral β tab tab deflection relative to the elevator Moving the elevator changes the tail’s aerodynamic loading. The tab can help balance the aerodynamic torque about the elevator hinge. A tail force L T and an elevator hinge torque H are different outputs. The following pages calculate each output separately. The subscript in β tab distinguishes the tab angle from aircraft sideslip, introduced later. 26"},{"source":"angles","page":27,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 4 / THE TAILPLANE Add incidence and control contributions to tail lift At a prescribed local incidence, add the contributions of the elevator and tab: C T L = a 1 α T + a 2 η + a 3 β tab , L T = 1 2 ρV 2 S T C T L . η (deg) C T L fixed α T larger α T a 1 α T −4 4 At fixed incidence, elevator deflection changes tail lift along a straight line. Increasing incidence shifts the line upwards. Coefficient Response when the other inputs stay fixed a 1 tail-incidence response a 2 elevator response a 3 tab response Use angles in radians with the supplied per-radian coefficients. All three contributions use tail area S T . The model takes zero lift at α T = η = β tab = 0. The first equation gives a coefficient; dynamic pressure and tail area turn it into a force. 27"},{"source":"angles","page":28,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 4 / THE TAILPLANE Hinge moment is a torque about the elevator hinge hinge aerodynamic load positive H: trailing edge down trailing edge load arm upward load aft of hinge tends to raise the trailing edge: negative H in this sketch The indicated elevator load turns about the hinge. It produces negative H under the positive trailing-edge-down convention. C H = b 1 α T + b 2 η + b 3 β tab , H = 1 2 ρV 2 S e ¯c e C H . The inputs are the same as for tail lift, but the b coefficients describe hinge torque. Use the elevator reference area S e and chord ¯c e supplied with those coefficients. 1 2 ρV 2 S e |  {z  } force scale ¯c e |{z} length scale −→ H in N m. For an elevator held stationary, the opposing holding torque is H hold = −H. A tab can reduce hinge torque while the tail continues to carry lift. Where does each quantity act: tail force L T , aircraft pitching moment M, and elevator hinge torque H? 28"},{"source":"angles","page":29,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes 4 / THE TAILPLANE Worked example: from wing incidence to tail load Given: C W L = 0.50, wing slope a = 4.8 1/rad, tail setting η T = −3 ◦ , downwash ε = 0.4α, and tail slope a 1 = 3.2 1/rad. Elevator and tab are neutral. ρ = 1.225 kg/m 3 , V = 80 m/s, S T = 10 m 2 . Assume the speed at the tail equals V. Find the tail lift. First predict whether its incidence is larger or smaller than the wing’s. wing incidence 5.97 ◦ setting −3.00 ◦ downwash −2.39 ◦ tail incidence 0.58 ◦ Use degrees to read the angle picture and unrounded radians for the lift calculation. α = 0.50 4.8 = 0.104167 rad, ε = 0.4α = 0.041667 rad, α T = α + η T − ε = 0.010140 rad, C T L = a 1 α T = 0.03245, 1 2 ρV 2 = 3920 Pa. L T = 1 2 ρV 2 S T C T L ≃ 3920(10)(0.03245) = 1272 N upwards. The small positive tail incidence is about 0.58 ◦ . The negative setting and downwash have removed most of the wing’s 5.97 ◦ incidence. 29"},{"source":"angles","page":30,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 2 Lecture notes Conclusions The complete calculation chain attitude and path wing incidence wing lift and wake tail angle and load The calculation now connects prescribed aircraft motion to wing and tail loads. wing incidence: α = θ + i w − γ, wing lift: C W L = aα, tail flow: ε = k ε α, tail incidence: α T = α + η T − ε, tail force: L T = 1 2 ρV 2 S T (a 1 α T + a 2 η + a 3 β tab ). If downwash increases at fixed wing incidence and control settings, which steps change, and what happens to tail lift? Week 3: force and moment equilibrium determine the loads the aircraft needs. These relations give the required angles and settings. 30"},{"source":"lecture4","page":1,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 4 Lecture notes STEADY LEVEL FLIGHT Week 4: Steady level flight and trim Find the elevator and tab settings that maintain steady level flight and remove the pilot’s holding effort. L = W, T = D, M = 0. Figure notation: L wb = L W , L T = L T , M wb 0 = M 0 , V e = V. The separate tail couple M T 0 is neglected. The pitching balance from Week 3 Take moments about the wing aerodynamic centre (AC), positive nose-up: M = M 0 + W(h − h 0 )¯c − L T l T . h and h 0 are the CG and wing-AC positions divided by the mean aerodynamic chord ¯c. The arm l T runs from wing AC to tail AC. C M = M 1 2 ρV 2 S W ¯c , C L = C W = W 1 2 ρV 2 S W . C M = C M0 + (h − h 0 )C L − ¯ V T C T L = 0. C M0 is the wing–body couple coefficient. C L uses wing area S W ; C T L uses tail area S T . 1"},{"source":"lecture4","page":2,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 4 Lecture notes 1 / PITCHING EQUILIBRIUM Tail volume and the required tail lift 1. Rearrange the pitching balance 0 = C M0 + (h − h 0 )C L − ¯ V T C T L C T L = h − h 0 ¯ V T C L + C M0 ¯ V T , ¯ V T = S T S W l T ¯c . Tail volume combines relative area and moment arm. The signed arm l T is positive aft of the wing AC. 2. Compare aft-tail and canard configurations aft tail S T S W l T > 0, ¯ V T > 0 nose h − h 0 C T L 0 intercept: C M0 / ¯ V T < 0 positive slope C L / ¯ V T canard S W S T l T < 0, ¯ V T < 0 nose h − h 0 C T L 0 intercept: C M0 / ¯ V T > 0 negative slope C L / ¯ V T For fixed C L > 0 and C M0 < 0: ∂C T L ∂(h − h 0 ) = C L ¯ V T , C T L h=h 0 = C M0 ¯ V T . A longer arm or larger tail area needs a smaller magnitude of tail coefficient for the same balancing moment. 2"},{"source":"lecture4","page":3,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 4 Lecture notes 2 / THE ELEVATOR AND TAB Tail lift and elevator hinge torque η β tab fixed tailplane elevator tab elevator hinge tab hinge +H α T local airflow Tail lift and hinge torque respond to the same three angles C T L = a 1 α T + a 2 η + a 3 β tab , C H = b 1 α T + b 2 η + b 3 β tab , C H = H 1 2 ρV 2 S e ¯c e . α T Tail incidence from its no-lift line to the local flow direction. η Elevator deflection relative to the fixed tailplane. β tab Tab deflection relative to the elevator. η and β tab are positive trailing edge down; positive H tends to increase η. a 1 , a 2 , a 3 are lift-response coefficients; b 1 , b 2 , b 3 are hinge-torque-response coefficients, here in 1/rad. S e and ¯c e are the elevator reference area and chord, including the tab for the supplied hinge data. 3"},{"source":"lecture4","page":4,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 4 Lecture notes 2 / THE ELEVATOR AND TAB Stick-fixed and stick-free trim The aircraft must balance in both cases. The difference is the constraint on the elevator. Stick-fixed Ideal stick-free The elevator angle is held. The elevator can rotate. The aircraft requires C M = 0. The aircraft requires C M = 0. A holding torque can balance the aero- dynamic hinge torque. No holding torque acts at the hinge. The tab is held at a selected setting, of- ten zero. The tab is held at a selected setting; the elevator floats. Balance the torques about the elevator hinge H + τ hold = 0. τ hold is the torque transmitted through the control connection. Stick-fixed: τ hold = −H, Ideal stick-free: τ hold = 0 =⇒ H = 0. Stick-fixed: C M = 0. Stick-free: C M = 0, C H = 0. A held elevator can also have C H = 0. In the ideal free case, friction, springs and other hinge torques are neglected. 4"},{"source":"lecture4","page":5,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 4 Lecture notes 3 / STICK-FIXED TRIM Stick-fixed trim: derive the elevator angle 1. Express tail incidence in terms of aircraft lift With C L = C W L + (S T /S W )C T L ≃ C W L = a w α and ϵ = (dϵ/dα)α: α T = α + η T − ϵ, = C L a w + η T − dϵ dα C L a w , = C L a w   1 − dϵ dα ! + η T . η T is the tail setting relative to the wing no-lift line. 2. Substitute into the tail lift law C T L = a 1 α T + a 2 η + a 3 β tab , = a 1 C L a w   1 − dϵ dα ! + a 1 η T + a 2 η + a 3 β tab . 3. Substitute into the pitching moment equation C M = C M0 + (h − h 0 )C L − ¯ V T C T L . C M = C M0 + (h − h 0 )C L − ¯ V T \" a 1 C L a w   1 − dϵ dα ! + a 1 η T + a 2 η + a 3 β tab # . 4. Set C M = 0 and isolate the elevator term a 2 η trim = C M0 ¯ V T + h − h 0 ¯ V T C L − a 1 C L a w   1 − dϵ dα ! − a 1 η T − a 3 β tab . Divide every term by a 2 to obtain η trim . 5"},{"source":"lecture4","page":6,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 4 Lecture notes 3 / STICK-FIXED TRIM Find the hinge torque at stick-fixed trim 1. Put the tail-incidence expression into the hinge law C H = b 1 α T + b 2 η + b 3 β tab , = b 1 \" C L a w   1 − dϵ dα ! + η T # + b 2 η + b 3 β tab , = b 1 a w   1 − dϵ dα ! C L + b 1 η T + b 2 η + b 3 β tab . 2. Write the trimmed elevator angle from page 5 η trim = C M0 a 2 ¯ V T + \" h − h 0 a 2 ¯ V T − a 1 a 2 a w   1 − dϵ dα !# C L − a 1 a 2 η T − a 3 a 2 β tab . 3. Substitute for η, then collect like terms C H,trim = b 1 a w   1 − dϵ dα ! C L + b 1 η T + b 3 β tab + b 2 C M0 a 2 ¯ V T + b 2 (h − h 0 ) a 2 ¯ V T C L − b 2 a 1 a 2 a w   1 − dϵ dα ! C L − b 2 a 1 a 2 η T − b 2 a 3 a 2 β tab . C H,trim = b 2 C M0 a 2 ¯ V T + \" b 2 (h − h 0 ) a 2 ¯ V T + 1 a w   b 1 − b 2 a 1 a 2 !   1 − dϵ dα !# C L +   b 1 − b 2 a 1 a 2 ! η T +   b 3 − b 2 a 3 a 2 ! β tab . H trim = 1 2 ρV 2 S e ¯c e C H,trim , τ hold = −H trim . 6"},{"source":"lecture4","page":7,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 4 Lecture notes 4 / STICK-FREE TRIM A free elevator changes the tail response 1. Let the elevator float: C H = 0 b 1 α T + b 2 η + b 3 β tab = 0 =⇒ η = − b 1 b 2 α T − b 3 b 2 β tab . Substitute α T = (C L /a w )(1 − dϵ/dα) + η T : η free = − \" b 1 C L b 2 a w   1 − dϵ dα ! + b 1 b 2 η T + b 3 b 2 β tab # . 2. Put this angle into the tail lift law C T L = a 1 α T + a 2 η + a 3 β tab = a 1 α T + a 2   − b 1 b 2 α T − b 3 b 2 β tab ! + a 3 β tab =   a 1 − a 2 b 1 b 2 ! α T +   a 3 − a 2 b 3 b 2 ! β tab . 3. Name the combined response coefficients ¯a 1 = a 1 − a 2 b 1 b 2 , ¯a 3 = a 3 − a 2 b 3 b 2 . C T L = ¯a 1 α T + ¯a 3 β tab . The barred coefficients include the lift change caused by elevator motion. Their values depend on the supplied derivative signs. 7"},{"source":"lecture4","page":8,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 4 Lecture notes 4 / STICK-FREE TRIM Stick-free trim: satisfy both moment balances 1. Use the free-elevator tail lift law C T L = ¯a 1 α T + ¯a 3 β tab , = ¯a 1 C L a w   1 − dϵ dα ! + ¯a 1 η T + ¯a 3 β tab . 2. Substitute into the aircraft pitching balance C M,free = C M0 + (h − h 0 )C L − ¯ V T C T L . C M,free = C M0 + (h − h 0 )C L − ¯ V T \" ¯a 1 C L a w   1 − dϵ dα ! + ¯a 1 η T + ¯a 3 β tab # . Here ¯a 1 = a 1 − a 2 b 1 /b 2 and ¯a 3 = a 3 − a 2 b 3 /b 2 . 3. Set C M,free = 0 and isolate the tab term ¯a 3 β tab,trim = C M0 ¯ V T + h − h 0 ¯ V T C L − ¯a 1 C L a w   1 − dϵ dα ! − ¯a 1 η T . Divide by ¯a 3 for the tab angle; use C H = 0 for the elevator angle: η free = − \" b 1 C L b 2 a w   1 − dϵ dα ! + b 1 b 2 η T + b 3 b 2 β tab,trim # . Alternatively, solve the two original laws together a 2 η + a 3 β tab = C T L − a 1 α T , b 2 η + b 3 β tab = −b 1 α T . The first supplies the required tail lift; the second imposes zero hinge torque. 8"},{"source":"lecture4","page":9,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 4 Lecture notes 5 / WORKED EXAMPLE Question 1: find the tail load and incidence Aircraft and aerodynamic data for Questions 1–3 ¯ V T = 0.8, C M0 = −0.01, h − h 0 = 0.1, C L = 1, η T = 0, dϵ dα = 0.4. a w a 1 a 2 a 3 b 1 b 2 b 3 5.7 5 4 0.5 0.11 −0.12 −0.05 All seven derivatives are in 1/rad. Question 1. Find the tail lift coefficient, downwash angle and tail incidence in steady level flight. 1. Use the aircraft pitching balance C T L = C M0 + (h − h 0 )C L ¯ V T = −0.01 + 0.1 · 1 0.8 ≃ 0.113. 2. Recover the wing incidence, then the downwash α ≃ C L a w = 1 5.7 ≃ 0.175 rad (10.05 ◦ ), ϵ = dϵ dα α = 0.4 5.7 ≃ 0.070 rad (4.02 ◦ ). 3. Find the incidence seen by the tail α T = α + η T − ϵ = 1 − 0.4 5.7 + 0 ≃ 0.105 rad (6.03 ◦ ). The tail needs positive lift, but downwash reduces its incidence below the wing inci- dence. 9"},{"source":"lecture4","page":10,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 4 Lecture notes 5 / WORKED EXAMPLE Question 2: trim with the tab at zero Question 2. With the tab at zero, find the elevator angle and hinge-torque coefficient at trim. C T L = −0.01 + 0.1 · 1 0.8 , α T = 0.6 5.7 rad, β tab = 0. 1. Set the elevator to produce the required tail coefficient C T L = a 1 α T + a 2 η + a 3 β tab , η trim = C T L − a 1 α T − a 3 β tab a 2 = (−0.01 + 0.1 · 1)/0.8 − 5 · (0.6/5.7) 4 ≃ −0.103 rad (−5.93 ◦ ). 2. Calculate the hinge torque at that same elevator angle C H,trim = b 1 α T + b 2 η trim + b 3 β tab = 0.11 · 0.6 5.7 − 0.12 · η trim ≃ 2.40 × 10 −2 . C T L incidence +0.526 elevator −0.414 total +0.113 The elevator moves up, reducing the positive tail lift to the required value. The hinge torque still needs an opposing holding torque. 10"},{"source":"lecture4","page":11,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 4 Lecture notes 5 / WORKED EXAMPLE Question 3: trim with no holding torque Question 3. Find the elevator and tab angles that preserve the required tail load with no holding torque. 1. Impose both the lift requirement and C H = 0 a 2 η + a 3 β tab = C T L − a 1 α T , b 2 η + b 3 β tab = −b 1 α T . α T = 0.6 5.7 , C T L = −0.01 + 0.1 · 1 0.8 . 4η + 0.5β tab = C T L − 5α T , −0.12η − 0.05β tab = −0.11α T . 2. Multiply the second equation by 10, then add −1.2η − 0.5β tab = −1.1α T , 2.8η = C T L − 6.1α T . η = C T L − 6.1α T 2.8 ≃ −0.189 rad (−10.84 ◦ ). 3. Recover the tab angle from the hinge equation β tab = 0.11α T − 0.12η 0.05 ≃ 0.686 rad (39.28 ◦ ). Using unrounded angles gives C T L ≃ 0.113 and C H = 0. The large tab angle is a formal linear-model result; it lies outside a credible small-deflection prediction. 11"},{"source":"lecture4","page":12,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 4 Lecture notes 6 / CHANGING THE FLIGHT CONDITION Flaps change the incidence needed for lift Keep α measured from the clean-wing no-lift line. α from clean zero-lift line C W L with lift offset clean ∆C L,flap 0 1. Include the additional wing lift at the same incidence C W L = a w α + ∆C L,flap . ∆C L,flap is the flap-induced lift offset in this local linear model. 2. Find the incidence for the required lift With C L ≃ C W L , α ≃ C L − ∆C L,flap a w . 3. Use that incidence in the tail geometry α T = α + η T − ϵ. Flaps can also change C M0 and downwash. Those changes require their own aerody- namic data before recalculating trim. 12"},{"source":"lecture4","page":13,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 4 Lecture notes 6 / CHANGING THE FLIGHT CONDITION Changed downwash changes the trim setting ϵ more downwash same tail setting ϵ less downwash same tail setting Consider a reduction in downwash, as can occur near the ground. Isolate this effect by holding α, η T , β tab and the required tail load fixed. 1. Find the change in tail incidence α T = α + η T − ϵ =⇒ ∆α T = −∆ϵ. 2. Keep the tail lift coefficient unchanged ∆C T L = a 1 ∆α T + a 2 ∆η = 0, a 2 ∆η = a 1 ∆ϵ. ∆η = a 1 a 2 ∆ϵ. Using a 1 = 5 1/rad, a 2 = 4 1/rad and ∆ϵ = −1 ◦ : ∆η = 5 4 · (−1 ◦ ) = −1.25 ◦ . Less downwash raises tail incidence. More up-elevator offsets that lift increase. 13"},{"source":"lecture4","page":14,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 4 Lecture notes Conclusions 1. The aircraft moment balance sets the required tail load C M = C M0 + (h − h 0 )C L − ¯ V T C T L = 0, ¯ V T = S T l T S W ¯c . Tail volume describes area and leverage, with the sign of the longitudinal arm. 2. Stick-fixed trim finds an elevator angle η trim = C T L − a 1 α T − a 3 β tab a 2 , C H = b 1 α T + b 2 η trim + b 3 β tab . The control connection supplies any required opposing hinge torque. 3. Stick-free trim also requires zero hinge torque a 2 η + a 3 β tab = C T L − a 1 α T , b 2 η + b 3 β tab = −b 1 α T . The selected tab and floating elevator must satisfy both equations. Calculation order: required tail load −→ tail incidence −→ elevator and tab −→ check both balances. The tutorial applies this sequence to different aircraft data and control settings. Trim establishes equilibrium. Week 5 examines stability: the pitching response after a small disturbance. 14"},{"source":"tut3","page":1,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and control Tutorial 3 typed solutions 3.1 η trim = C m 0 V T a ₂ − a 1 η T a ₂ + C L ¯ V T a ₂ [ ( h − h ₀ ) − ¯ V T a ₁ a ( 1 − dε dα ) ] η T = a ₂ a ₁ { C m 0 ¯ V T a ₂ − η trim + C L ¯ V T a ₂ [ ( h − h ₀ ) − ¯ V T a ₁ a ( 1 − dε dα ) ] } η T = 1.2 2.8 { − 0.016 0.48 · 1.2 − 0 + 0.3 0.48 · 1.2 [ 0.09 − 0.48 1.8 4.5 ( 1 − 0.4 ) ] } = − 0.0318  rad = − 1.82 0 h = h ₀ + ¯ V T a ₁ a ( 1 − dε dα ) + ¯ V T a ₂ C L [ η trim − C m 0 ¯ V T a ₂ + a 1 η T a 2 ] h = 0.18 + 0.48 · 2.8 · 0.6 4.5 + 0.48 · 1.2 1.4 ( − 30 · π 180 + 0.016 0.48 · 1.2 − 2.8 ⋅ 0.0318 1.2 ) = 0.125 3.2 (i) C m = C m 0 + ( h − h ₀ ) C L − ¯ V T C L T    (from lecture, pitching moment equation) C m = 0 C L = − C m 0 h − h ₀ − ¯ V T C L T / C L ¯ V T = S T l T S ¯ ¯ c = 5 ⋅ 10 40 ⋅ 2 = 5 8 S T ⋅ C L T S ⋅ C L = L T L C L T C L = L T mg S S T = 0.01 40 5 = 0.08 C L = 0.1 0.1 − 0.08 ⋅ 5 / 8 = 2 C L T = 0.16 (ii) ε = dε / dα · C L = 0.1 · 2 = 0.2 rad = 11.46 ° α T = α + η T − ε = C L / a + η T − ε α T = 2 / 4.5 · 180 / π − 3 − 11.46 = 11 0 1"},{"source":"tut3","page":2,"text":"(iii) C L T = a ₁ α T + a ₂ η + a ₃ β β = 0 ⇒ η = ( C L T − a ₁ α T ) / a ₂ η = ( 0.16 − 2.8 · 11 · π / 180 ) / 1.2 = − 0.315   rad  = − 18.03 0 (iv) C L T = a ₁ α T + a ₂ η + a ₃ β C H = b ₁ α T + b ₂ η + b ₃ β ,    C H = 0 η = − ( b ₁ α T + b ₃ β ) / b ₂ C L T = ( a ₁ − a ₂ b ₁ / b ₂ ) α T + ( a ₃ − a ₂ b ₃ / b ₂ ) β ¯ a 1 = a ₁ − a ₂ b ₁ / b ₂ = 3.8 ,   ¯ a 2 = a ₃ − a ₂ b ₃ / b ₂ = 2.7 β = ( C L T − ¯ a ₁ α T ) / ¯ a ₂ = ( 0.16 − 3.8 · 11 · π / 180 ) / ( − 2.7 ) = 0.211  rad  = 12.09 0 η = ( 0.01 · 11 · π / 180 − 0.03 · 0.211 ) / 0.012 = − 0.368  rad  = − 21.06 0 3.3 (i) C L = − C m 0 h − h ₀ − ¯ V T C L T / C L = 0.2 0.1 − 5 ⋅ 0.08 / 8 = 4 C L T = 4 ⋅ 0.08 = 0.32 (ii) ε = dε / dα · C L = 0.1 · 4 = 0.4 rad = 22.9 ° α T = α + η T − ε = 0.8 ⋅ C L / a + η T − ε α T = 0.8 ⋅ 4 / 4.5 · 180 / π − 3 − 22.9 = 14.84 0 (iii) η = ( C L T − a ₁ α T ) / a ₂ η = ( 0.32 − 2.8 · 14.84 · π / 180 ) / 1.2 = − 0.338   rad  = − 19.35 0 (iv) β = ( C L T − ¯ a ₁ α T ) / ¯ a ₂ = ( 0.32 − 3.8 · 14.84 · π / 180 ) / ( − 2.7 ) = 0.246  rad  = 14.1 0 η = − ( b ₁ α T + b ₃ β ) / b ₂ η = ( 0.01 · 14.84 · π / 180 − 0.03 · 0.246 ) / 0.012 = − 0.399  rad  = − 22.87 0 2"},{"source":"tut3","page":3,"text":"3.4 H n ' = h ₀ − h + ¯ V T ¯ a ₁ a ( 1 − dε dα ) ¯ V T = H n ' − ( h ₀ − h ) ¯ a ₁ / a · ( 1 − dε / dα ) ¯ a ₁ = a ₁ − a ₂ b ₁ b ₂ = 3 − 1.8 · 0.01 − 0.06 = 3.3 ¯ V T = 0.12 + 0.18 3.3 / 4.8 · ( 1 − 0.47 ) = 0.823 ¯ V T = S T l T S ¯ ¯ c S T = ¯ V T S ¯ ¯ c l T = 0.823 · 10 · 1.15 10 = 1.23 m ² 3.5 C m = C m 0 + ( h − h ₀ ) C L − ¯ V T [ ¯ a ₁ a · ( 1 − dε dα ) C L + ¯ a ₁ η + ¯ a ₃ β ] C m = 0 ¯ a ₃ β trim = C m 0 + ( h − h ₀ ) C L ¯ V T − ¯ a ₁ a ( 1 − dε dα ) C L − ¯ a ₁ η T β trim = ( C m 0 ¯ V T ¯ a ₃ − ¯ a ₁ η T ¯ a ₃ ) + ( h − h ₀ ) C L ¯ a ₃ ¯ V T − ¯ a ₁ a ¯ a ₃ ( 1 − dε dα ) C L β trim = ( C m 0 ¯ V T ¯ a ₃ − ¯ a ₁ η T ¯ a ₃ ) − C L ¯ V T ¯ a 3 [ ( h − h ₀ ) + ¯ V T ¯ a ₁ a ( 1 − dε dα ) ] β trim = ( C m 0 ¯ V T ¯ a ₃ − ¯ a ₁ η T ¯ a ₃ ) − C L ¯ V Tbar a 3 H ' n 3.6 β new − β old = − C L , new − C L , old ¯ V T a ₃ ā ₃ = a ₃ − a ₂ b ₃ b ₂ = 0.5 − 2.3 · ( − 0.08 ) − 0.15 = 0.454 C L , old = W 0.5 ρ U ∞ 2 S = 3000 0.5 · 0.6 · 180² · 1.225 = 0.248 C L , new = 3000 0.5 · 0.74 · 85² · 1.225 = 0.916 3"},{"source":"tut3","page":4,"text":"β new = − ( 0.916 − 0.278 ) · 0.06 0.55 · 0.454 = 0.161  rad  = − 9.2 0 3.7  (Pull-out manoeuvre, not in this year assessment, but we will experience it in the flight course) Δη η ⋅ ¯ V T a ₂ C L ₁ = h − h ₀ − ¯ V T [ a ₁ a ( 1 − dε dα ) + a ₁ 2 μ ] μ = m ρ S l T = 65000 1.225 · 190 · 14 = 19.95 C L ₁ = mg ½ ρ V ² e S = 65000 · 9.8 ½ · 1.225 · 150² · 190 = 0.2433 Δη = n C L ₁ ¯ V T a ₂ { h − h ₀ − ¯ V T [ a ₁ a ( 1 − dε dα ) + a ₁ 2 μ ] } Δη = 2.5 ⋅ 0.2433 0.49 ⋅ 2 { 0.25 − 0.16 − 0.49 [ 3.7 4.5 ( 1 − 0.49 ) + 3.7 2 ⋅ 19.95 ] } = − 0.100  rad  = − 5.72 0 E Avital 4"},{"source":"equilibrium","page":1,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 3 Lecture notes 1 / STEADY LEVEL FLIGHT Week 3: Equilibrium Write and use the force and moment equations for steady level flight, climb, descent and glide. Steady level flight: constant speed and height V, flight path horizontal normal L D T W V: airspeed; L: total lift; D: drag; T : thrust; W = mg: weight. Along the flight path: T − D = 0, Normal to the flight path: L − W = 0. L = W, T = D. Pitching equilibrium M = 0. M is the net pitching moment. Nose-up is positive. The aircraft is trimmed when its forces and pitching moment balance at the chosen flight condition. 1"},{"source":"equilibrium","page":2,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 3 Lecture notes 1 / STEADY LEVEL FLIGHT Total lift is shared by the wing and tail Lift is the combined contribution of the wing–body and the tailplane. Take both loads as positive upwards in level flight. L W L T CG W 1. Add the forces L = L W + L T . Using the same density ρ and speed V at both surfaces, L = 1 2 ρV 2 S W C L , L W = 1 2 ρV 2 S W C W L , L T = 1 2 ρV 2 S T C T L . 2. Divide by the whole-aircraft reference force L 1 2 ρV 2 S W = L W 1 2 ρV 2 S W + L T 1 2 ρV 2 S T S T S W . C L = C W L + S T S W C T L . S W and S T are wing and tail planform areas. The area ratio appears because the two surface coefficients use different reference areas. 2"},{"source":"equilibrium","page":3,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 3 Lecture notes 1 / STEADY LEVEL FLIGHT Differentiate the lift equation at fixed controls Geometry, tail setting and controls are fixed. The lift-response coefficients a w , a 1 , a 2 , a 3 are constant, in 1/rad. α and α T are wing and tail incidence from their no-lift lines. 1. Differentiate the total lift relation C L = C W L + S T S W C T L =⇒ ∂C L ∂α = ∂C W L ∂α + S T S W ∂C T L ∂α . 2. Differentiate the wing contribution a w is the finite-wing lift slope called a in Week 2. C W L = a w α =⇒ ∂C W L ∂α = ∂(a w α) ∂α = a w . 3. Differentiate the tail contribution C T L = a 1 α T + a 2 η + a 3 β tab , ∂C T L ∂α = a 1 ∂α T ∂α + a 2 ∂η ∂α |{z} 0 +a 3 ∂β tab ∂α |{z} 0 . The elevator angle η and tab angle β tab are held fixed. From Week 2, α T = α + η T − ε =⇒ ∂α T ∂α = 1 + ∂η T ∂α |{z} 0 − dε dα . The tail setting η T is fixed; downwash ε changes with α. Hence ∂C T L ∂α = a 1   1 − dε dα ! . 4. Substitute both contributions ∂C L ∂α = a w + S T S W a 1   1 − dε dα ! . Neglecting the tail contribution gives ∂C L /∂α ≃ a w . 3"},{"source":"equilibrium","page":4,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 3 Lecture notes 1 / STEADY LEVEL FLIGHT Take moments about the wing lift application point Figure notation: L wb = L W , L T = L T , M wb 0 = M 0 , V e = V. The separate tail couple M T 0 is neglected. 1. Read the positions and arms ¯c is the wing mean aerodynamic chord. The CG and wing AC lie h¯c and h 0 ¯c aft of its leading edge. Their separation is (h − h 0 )¯c. l T is the arm from the wing aerodynamic centre (AC) to the tail AC. 2. Take moments about the wing lift application point M is the net moment about the wing AC; nose-up is positive. M = M 0 + W(h¯c − h 0 ¯c) − L T l T = M 0 + W(h − h 0 )¯c − L T l T . Wing lift has zero arm about this point. Its aerodynamic couple M 0 remains. 3. Define the moment coefficient C M = M 1 2 ρV 2 S W ¯c . 4"},{"source":"equilibrium","page":5,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 3 Lecture notes 1 / STEADY LEVEL FLIGHT Express the pitching balance in coefficients 1. Start from the same dimensional equation M = M 0 + W(h − h 0 )¯c − L T l T . 2. Divide every term by 1 2 ρV 2 S W ¯c M 1 2 ρV 2 S W ¯c |      {z      } C M = M 0 1 2 ρV 2 S W ¯c |      {z      } C M0 + W(h − h 0 )¯c 1 2 ρV 2 S W ¯c − L T l T 1 2 ρV 2 S W ¯c . Weight term: cancel ¯c. W(h − h 0 )¯c 1 2 ρV 2 S W ¯c = W 1 2 ρV 2 S W |    {z    } C W (h − h 0 ). Tail term: insert S T /S T = 1. L T l T 1 2 ρV 2 S W ¯c = L T 1 2 ρV 2 S T |    {z    } C T L S T l T S W ¯c |{z} ¯ V T . Thus C M = C M0 + C W (h − h 0 ) − C T L ¯ V T , ¯ V T = S T l T S W ¯c . ¯ V T is the dimensionless tailplane volume coefficient. 3. Use the level-flight force balance L = W =⇒ L 1 2 ρV 2 S W = W 1 2 ρV 2 S W =⇒ C L = C W . C M = C M0 + C L (h − h 0 ) − C T L ¯ V T . Pitching equilibrium requires M = 0, hence C M = 0. 5"},{"source":"equilibrium","page":6,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 3 Lecture notes 1 / STEADY LEVEL FLIGHT Can the tailplane volume coefficient be negative? Raytheon–Beech canard configuration. 1. Keep the same meaning for the reference areas S W is the main-wing area; S T is the aft-tail or foreplane area. Both areas and ¯c are positive. 2. Give the longitudinal arm a sign Measure l T from the main-wing AC to the second surface’s AC, positive aft: aft tail: ¯ V T > 0 l T > 0 S T S W nose canard: ¯ V T < 0 l T < 0 S W S T nose 3. Read the moment contribution ¯ V T = S T l T S W ¯c , second-surface moment coefficient = −C T L ¯ V T . An upward force on an aft tail gives a nose-down contribution. An upward force on a foreplane gives a nose-up contribution. The signed arm accounts for that change. 6"},{"source":"equilibrium","page":7,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 3 Lecture notes 1 / STEADY LEVEL FLIGHT Calculate tail lift for pitching equilibrium Question. In steady level flight, an aircraft has ¯ V T = 0.8, h − h 0 = 0.2, C M0 = −0.05, C L = 1. Find the tail lift coefficient required for pitching equilibrium. 1. Solve for the unknown before inserting numbers 0 = C M0 + C L (h − h 0 ) − C T L ¯ V T , C T L ¯ V T = C M0 + C L (h − h 0 ), C T L = C M0 + C L (h − h 0 ) ¯ V T . 2. Substitute and interpret the sign C T L = −0.05 + 1 · 0.2 0.8 ≃ +0.188. Moment contributions: C M0 = −0.05, C L (h − h 0 ) = +0.20, and −C T L ¯ V T = −0.15. CG L T > 0 nose-down nose The positive tail coefficient gives an upward tail load and a nose-down moment. Second question. What is the weight coefficient in this level-flight condition? C W ≡ W 1 2 ρV 2 S W = C L = 1. 7"},{"source":"equilibrium","page":8,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 3 Lecture notes 2 / STEADY CLIMB Steady climb: resolve the forces Steady climb: constant speed along a straight path at angle γ > 0 to the horizontal. horizontal V, flight path normal L D T W γ Lift is normal to V. Drag opposes V; thrust acts along V. Weight remains vertical. 1. Resolve weight in flight-path axes Along the path: − W sin γ, Normal to the path: − W cos γ. 2. Apply force equilibrium T − D − W sin γ = 0 =⇒ T = D + W sin γ, L − W cos γ = 0 =⇒ L = W cos γ. 3. Express the lift requirement as a coefficient C L = L 1 2 ρV 2 S W = W cos γ 1 2 ρV 2 S W . C L = C W cos γ, C W = W 1 2 ρV 2 S W . For γ = 0, these equations give T = D and L = W. 8"},{"source":"equilibrium","page":9,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 3 Lecture notes 2 / STEADY CLIMB Steady climb: write the pitching moment The notation and moment reference point are the same as on page 4. γ is the flight-path angle. 1. Take moments about the wing lift application point The normal weight component is now W cos γ: M = M 0 + W cos γ (h − h 0 )¯c − L T l T . 2. Divide by the same reference moment C M = C M0 + W cos γ (h − h 0 )¯c 1 2 ρV 2 S W ¯c − L T l T 1 2 ρV 2 S W ¯c = C M0 + C W cos γ (h − h 0 ) − C T L ¯ V T . Since C L = C W cos γ, C M = C M0 + C L (h − h 0 ) − C T L ¯ V T . Question. How must C T L vary with γ to keep C M = 0, if C W is constant? Take C M0 and the geometry as fixed. 9"},{"source":"equilibrium","page":10,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 3 Lecture notes 2 / STEADY CLIMB Find how tail lift changes with climb angle 1. Set the climbing moment coefficient to zero 0 = C M0 + C W (h − h 0 ) cos γ − C T L (γ) ¯ V T , C T L (γ) ¯ V T = C M0 + C W (h − h 0 ) cos γ, C T L (γ) = C M0 + C W (h − h 0 ) cos γ ¯ V T . 2. Write the level-flight value, γ = 0 C T L (0) = C M0 + C W (h − h 0 ) ¯ V T . 3. Subtract the level-flight value C T L (γ) − C T L (0) = C M0 + C W (h − h 0 ) cos γ − C M0 − C W (h − h 0 ) ¯ V T = C W (h − h 0 )(cos γ − 1) ¯ V T . C T L (γ) = C T L (0) + C W (h − h 0 ) ¯ V T (cos γ − 1). Special case: C M0 = 0 C T L (0) = C W (h − h 0 ) ¯ V T =⇒ C T L (γ) = C T L (0) cos γ. For nonzero C M0 , use the general result above. 10"},{"source":"equilibrium","page":11,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 3 Lecture notes 3 / STEADY DESCENT Steady descent Steady descent: γ < 0. horizontal V, flight path normal L D T W |γ| 1. Use the same signed force equations T − D − W sin γ = 0, L − W cos γ = 0. 2. Use sin γ = − sin |γ| and cos γ = cos |γ| T = D − W sin |γ|, L = W cos |γ|. Weight now supplies part of the force needed to overcome drag. 3. Apply pitching equilibrium C L = C W cos |γ|, 0 = C M0 + C W (h − h 0 ) cos |γ| − C T L ¯ V T , C T L = C M0 + C W (h − h 0 ) cos |γ| ¯ V T . At fixed C W , C M0 and geometry, equal climb and descent angle magnitudes give the same C L and C T L . Their thrust requirements differ. 11"},{"source":"equilibrium","page":12,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 3 Lecture notes 4 / GLIDE Glide A steady glide is a descent with T = 0. horizontal V, flight path normal L D W |γ| 1. Set thrust to zero in the descent equations 0 = D − W sin |γ| =⇒ D = W sin |γ|, L = W cos |γ|. 2. Divide to find the glide angle D L = W sin |γ| W cos |γ| = tan |γ|. γ = − tan −1 D L . A larger lift-to-drag ratio gives a shallower steady glide. 3. The aircraft must also balance in pitch C L = C W cos γ, 0 = C M0 + C L (h − h 0 ) − C T L ¯ V T , C T L = C M0 + C L (h − h 0 ) ¯ V T . 12"},{"source":"equilibrium","page":13,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 3 Lecture notes Conclusions Flight condition Normal force balance Along-path balance Level, γ = 0 L = W T = D Climb, γ > 0 L = W cos γ T = D + W sin γ Descent, γ < 0 L = W cos |γ| T = D − W sin |γ| Glide, T = 0 L = W cos |γ| D = W sin |γ| Lift coefficients C L = C W cos γ, C W = W 1 2 ρV 2 S W , C L = C W L + S T S W C T L . Pitching moment and trim C M = C M0 + C L (h − h 0 ) − C T L ¯ V T , ¯ V T = S T l T S W ¯c . For C M = 0, C T L = C M0 + C L (h − h 0 ) ¯ V T . Calculation order Flight condition −→ (L, T ) −→ C L −→ C T L . 13"},{"source":"tut1","page":1,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and control Tutorial 1 typed solutions 1.1 a = C L W α − α 0 α = C L W a + α 0 = 0.5 4.8 − 1.2 π 180 α = 0.0832 rad = 4.77 0 1.2 θ = pitch angle γ  = climbing angle Relationship: α + γ = i w + θ Solving for θ: θ = α − i w + γ = 4.77 0 − 3 0 + 0 0 Final result: θ = 1.77 0 1.3 Force equation normal to  U ∞ L T + L W = W ∗ cos ( γ ) L T = 1 2 ρ U ∞ 2 S T C L T L W = 1 2 ρ ⋅ U ∞ 2 S W C L W U ∞ , ρ , S T , W  stay the same as when  γ was varied Therefore C L W ( γ ) = C L W ( γ = 0 ) ∗ cos ( γ ) = 0.485 (i) From 1.1 1"},{"source":"tut1","page":2,"text":"α = C L W a + α 0 = 0.485 4.8 − 1.2 π 180 α = 0.082 rad = 4.71 0 (ii) From 1.2 θ = α − i w + γ = 4.71 0 − 3 0 + 8 0 = 9.71 0 1.4 Throttle must be increased in order to overcome the contribution of the weight on the direction of   U ∞  direction, i.e.  T new = D + W ∗ sin ( γ ) ,  T  – thrust,  D  – drag 1.5 Moment equation around the tail plane M 0 + L W ∗ l T = W ∗ l 1 divide the equation by  1 2 ρ U ∞ 2 S W ¯ ¯ c C m 0 + C L W ∗ ( l T / ¯ ¯ c ) = C W ∗ ( l 1 / ¯ ¯ c ) Solving for C W : C W = ( ¯ ¯ c / l 1 ) ∗ [ C m 0 + C L W ( l T / ¯ ¯ c ) ] C W = ( 2.5 / 7.5 ) ∗ ( − 0.037 + 0.5 ∗ 7.5 / 2.5 ) C W = 0.523  for  C L W = 0.5 C W = 0.518  for  C L W = 0.485 1.6 Lift coefficient definition: L W = 1 2 ρ U ∞ 2 S W C L W Weight coefficient definition: W = 1 2 ρ U ∞ 2 S W C W Therefore: L W / W = C L W / C W L W = W ∗ ( C L W / C W ) = 10,000 ∗ 9.81 ∗ 0.5 / 0.523 = 93.7 kN 2"},{"source":"tut1","page":3,"text":"1.7 (i) L W + L T = W L T = W − L W = L 10,000 ∗ 9.81 − 93.7 × 10 3 = 4.41 kN (ii) Divide the force equation above by  ( 1 / 2 ρ U ∞ 2 S W ) C L W + C L T ∗ ( S T / S W ) = C W Rearrange C L T = ( S W / S T ) ∗ ( C W − C L W ) C L W = 0.5  as already known, hence C L T = ( 50 / 10 ) ∗ ( 0.523 − 0.5 ) = 0.115 1.8 C W = W / ( 1 / 2 ρ U ∞ 2 S W ) ⇒ C W new = C W old ∗ ( U ∞ old / U ∞ new ) 2 = 0.523 / ( 1.2 ) 2 = 0.363 From 1.5 — Moment equation around the tail C m 0 + C L W ∗ ( l T / ¯ ¯ c ) = C W ∗ ( l 1 / ¯ ¯ c ) 0.03 + C L W ∗ ( 8 / 2.5 ) = 0.363 ∗ ( 7.5 / 2.5 ) C L W = 0.35 α = C L W a + α 0 = 0.35 4.8 − 1.2 π 180 α = 0.052  rad  = 2.98 0 Force equation: C L T = ( S W / S T ) ∗ ( C W − C L W ) = ( 50 / 10 ) ∗ ( 0.363 − 0.35 ) = 0.065 Pitch angle: θ = α − i w + γ = 2.98 0 − 3 0 + 0 0 = − 0.02 0 1.9  Not solved this year (see handwritten solutions) E Avital 3"},{"source":"tut2","page":1,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and control Tutorial 2 typed solutions 2.1 Data S − wing area AR − aspect ratio λ = c t / c 0 b − span 0 ⩽ k ⩽ 1 (i) S = ( c 0 + c t ) / 2 ∗ b AR = b 2 / S S = c 0 ( 1 + λ ) b / 2 = c 0 ( 1 + λ ) / 2 ∗ √ AR ∗ S Hence c 0 = 2 / ( 1 + λ ) ∗ √ ( S / AR ) (ii) BC = AC − AB = BD − CD ( b / 2 ) ( tan Λ 0 − tan Λ k ) = k c 0 − k c t ( b / 2 ) ( tan Λ 0 − tan Λ k ) = k c 0 ( 1 − c t / c 0 ) = k c 0 ( 1 − λ ) Rearrange tan Λ k = tan Λ 0 − 2 k c 0 ( 1 − λ ) / b Subsitiute  c 0 = 2 / ( 1 + λ ) ∗ √ ( S / AR ) b = √ AR ∗ S And we get tan Λ k = tan Λ 0 − 4 k ( 1 − λ ) / [ AR ( 1 + λ ) ] (iii) c varies linearly c = c 0 + ( c t − c 0 ) / ( b / 2 ) ∗ y ¯ ¯ c = ∫ 0 b / 2 c 2 dy ∫ 0 b / 2 c dy ∫ 0 b / 2 c dy = ( c t + c 0 ) b / 4 = c 0 ( 1 + λ ) b / 4 ∫ 0 b / 2 c 2 dy = ∫ 0 b / 2 [ c 0 + ( c t − c 0 ) / ( b / 2 ) ∗ y ] 2 dy ∫ 0 b / 2 c 2 dy = c 0 2 ∗ ( b / 2 ) + c 0 ( c t − c 0 ) ∗ ( b / 2 ) + ( c t − c 0 ) 2 ∗ ( b / 6 ) ¯ ¯ c = 2 c 0 [ 1 + λ − 1 + ( λ − 1 ) 2 / 3 ] 1 + λ = 2 c 0 ( 3 λ + λ 2 − 2 λ + 1 ) 3 ( 1 + λ ) ¯ ¯ c = 2 c 0 ( 1 + λ + λ 2 ) 3 ( 1 + λ ) 1"},{"source":"tut2","page":2,"text":"(iv) c = c ₀ + ( c t − c ₀ ) ∗ ( y / ( b / 2 ) ) c b = c ( y = d / 2 ) = c ₀ + ( c t − c ₀ ) ∗ d / 2 b / 2 = c 0 [ 1 + ( λ − 1 ) d b ] c b = c ₀ [ 1 − ( 1 − λ ) d b ] 2.2 Data: S = 0.25 m ² S δ = 0.06 m ² ¯ ¯ c δ = 0.04 m       a ₁ = 3.5 1 / rad a ₂ = 1.7 1 / rad a ₃ = 0.3 1 / rad       b ₁ = 0.03 1 / rad b ₂ = − 0.014 1 / rad b ₃ = − 0.03 1 / rad α ₀ = 0 ,   α = 3 ° ,   δ = − 4 ° ,   β = 5 ° ,   b ₀ = 0 Solution: (i) Lift C L = a ₁ ( α ₁ − α ₀ ) + a ₂ δ + a ₃ β = ( 3.5 · 3 + 1.7 · ( − 4 ) + 0.3 · 5 ) · π / 180 = 0.09 L = 1 / 2 ρ U ∞ 2 S C L = 0.5 · 1.225 · 60² · 0.25 · 0.09 = 49.6 N Torque C H = b ₀ + b ₁ ( α ₁ − α ₀ ) + b ₂ δ + b ₃ β = 0 + ( 0.09 · 3 + 0.014 · 4 − 0.03 · 5 ) · π / 180 = 3.072 ∗ 10 − 3 H = 1 / 2 ρ U ∞ 2 S δ ¯ ¯ c δ C H = 0.5 ∗ 1.225 ∗ 60 2 ∗ 0.06 ∗ 0.04 ∗ 3.072 ∗ 10 − 3 = 0.0163 N m (ii) C H = b ₀ + b ₁ ( α ₁ − α ₀ ) + b ₂ δ + b ₃ β = 0 + ( 0.09 · 3 − 0.014 · δ − 0.03 · 5 ) · π / 180 = 0 δ = 8.57 0 (iii) C L = a ₁ ( α ₁ − α ₀ ) + a ₂ δ + a ₃ β = ( 3.5 · 3 + 1.7 · 8.57 + 0.3 · 5 ) · π / 180 = 0.464 L = 1 / 2 ρ U ∞ 2 S C L = 0.5 · 1.225 · 60² · 0.25 · 0.464 = 255.6 N E Avital 2"},{"source":"lecture5a","page":1,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 5 Part 1 PART 1 / STATIC LONGITUDINAL STABILITY From aircraft trim to static stability Week 4 found the elevator and tab settings for balanced flight. The next question is whether a small incidence disturbance produces a restoring pitching moment. C M = 0 at trim, ∆α > 0 =⇒ ∆C M < 0 for a restoring response. Trim Forces and moments balance at the chosen flight condi- tion. Static stability A small disturbance produces a moment opposing that disturbance. The sequence in this part is: 1. Define the condition for longitudinal static stability. 2. Derive the stick-fixed and stick-free CG margins. 3. Calculate both margins using the stated aircraft data. 4. Relate the margins to elevator and hinge-torque trim gradients. Part 2 connects these gradients to stick movement and pilot force. 1"},{"source":"lecture5a","page":2,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 5 Part 1 1 / THE STABILITY TEST The condition for longitudinal static stability Start from trim. Increase wing incidence α slightly, without retrimming. If the air- relative flight-path direction is initially unchanged, a small nose-up rotation gives this increase. From Week 2, i w is the fixed wing no-lift-line setting relative to the fuselage datum: α = θ + i w − γ, ∆γ ≃ 0 =⇒ ∆α ≃ ∆θ. ∆C L ∆C M stable unstable neutral trim more lift → nose-down increment on the stable line Increment coordinates: the origin represents the initial trim point. Positive C M is nose-up. Controls must obey the stated fixed or free constraint. ∆C M ≃ dC M dα ∆α, ∆C L ≃ dC L dα ∆α. For a positive lift slope, the two equivalent tests are dC M dα < 0 ⇐⇒ dC M dC L < 0. At fixed speed and density, dM/dL = ¯c dC M /dC L has the same sign. A negative incidence change then produces a nose-up restoring moment. This is a static tendency. Whether the later motion decays or oscillates requires inertia, damping and the dynamic equations. Zero slope is neutral in this first-order test. 2"},{"source":"lecture5a","page":3,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 5 Part 1 1 / CG POSITION AND PITCHING MOMENT Start with the wing and tail moment balance h is the aircraft CG position; h 0 is the wing AC position. Both are measured aft from the mean aerodynamic chord’s leading edge and divided by ¯c. L W L T W wing AC CG tail AC l T (AC to AC) (h − h 0 )¯c nose +M ¯ V T = S T l T S W ¯c , C M = C M 0 + (h − h 0 )C L − ¯ V T C T L . Week 3 retained both lift contributions. The original Week 5 model uses C L = C W L + S T S W C T L ≃ C W L = aα, a = a w . Tail lift is retained in the moment balance. From Weeks 2–4, α T = α + η T − ϵ = C L a 1 − dϵ dα + η T . C T L = a 1 α T + a 2 η + a 3 β, C H = b 1 α T + b 2 η + b 3 β. α uses the wing no-lift line; α T (also written α T ) uses the tail no-lift line. η T is tail set- ting; η is elevator deflection; β = β tab is tab deflection relative to the elevator. Positive control angles are trailing edge down. The a i , b i are per-radian derivatives. 3"},{"source":"lecture5a","page":4,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 5 Part 1 2 / STICK-FIXED STABILITY Hold the elevator and find the restoring slope Constraint: elevator η and tab β are fixed during the incidence disturbance. Tail setting and aerodynamic derivatives also stay fixed. Tail incidence and tail lift can still change. C M = C M 0 + (h − h 0 )C L − ¯ V T a 1 C L a 1 − dϵ dα + a 1 η T + a 2 η + a 3 β . Constant terms have zero derivative; the derivative of each linear C L term is its multi- plying coefficient: dC M dC L η,β = (h − h 0 ) − ¯ V T a 1 a (1 − dϵ dα ). Define the stick-fixed CG margin, also called the static margin: H n ≡ − dC M dC L η,β = (h 0 − h) + ¯ V T a 1 a (1 − dϵ dα ). The corresponding neutral point is the CG location giving zero slope: h n = h 0 + ¯ V T a 1 a (1 − dϵ dα ), H n = h n − h. CG position Margin Static response h < h n H n > 0 restoring h = h n H n = 0 neutral h > h n H n < 0 destabilising Increasing tail volume strengthens the restoring contribution when a 1 > 0 and dϵ dα < 1. Moving the CG aft reduces the margin. 4"},{"source":"lecture5a","page":5,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 5 Part 1 2 / STICK-FREE CG MARGIN Let the elevator float and differentiate The tab stays fixed. The elevator is free to settle at zero aerodynamic hinge torque: 0 = b 1 α T + b 2 η + b 3 β =⇒ η = − b 1 b 2 α T − b 3 b 2 β. Substitute into the tail lift law and collect terms: C T L = a 1 α T + a 2 − b 1 b 2 α T − b 3 b 2 β + a 3 β = ¯a 1 α T + ¯a 3 β, ¯a 1 = a 1 − a 2 b 1 b 2 , ¯a 3 = a 3 − a 2 b 3 b 2 . C M,free = C M 0 + (h − h 0 )C L − ¯ V T ¯a 1 C L a 1 − dϵ dα + ¯a 1 η T + ¯a 3 β . At fixed tab, the terms independent of C L disappear on differentiation: dC M dC L C H =0,β = (h − h 0 ) − ¯ V T ¯a 1 a 1 − dϵ dα . H ′ n = − dC M dC L = (h 0 − h) + ¯ V T ¯a 1 a 1 − dϵ dα . The prime denotes stick-free. H ′ n > 0 is restoring; H ′ n = 0 is neutral. The bar includes elevator movement; it does not mean η = 0. 5"},{"source":"lecture5a","page":6,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 5 Part 1 2 / COMPARING THE TWO MARGINS The hinge derivatives decide which margin is larger Both margins measure the CG distance ahead of a neutral point. The neutral point represents the incremental lift response under its specified control constraint. aft h 0 h h n h ′ n wing AC CG H n H ′ n chordwise positions divided by ¯c ordering shown for b 1 > 0 Subtract the stick-fixed margin from the stick-free margin: H ′ n − H n = ¯ V T a 1 − dϵ dα (¯a 1 − a 1 ) = − ¯ V T a 1 − dϵ dα a 2 b 1 b 2 . For an aft tail, take ¯ V T , a, a 2 > 0, dϵ dα < 1 and b 2 < 0: Hinge response Effective tail slope Margin ordering b 1 > 0 ¯a 1 > a 1 H ′ n > H n b 1 = 0 ¯a 1 = a 1 H ′ n = H n b 1 < 0 ¯a 1 < a 1 H ′ n < H n For b 1 > 0, increased tail incidence floats the elevator down and adds to the tail lift increment. For b 1 < 0, it floats up and reduces that increment. The ordering follows from the derivatives. Releasing an elevator does not universally increase or decrease static stability. 6"},{"source":"lecture5a","page":7,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 5 Part 1 2 / WORKED EXAMPLE Calculate both margins for the same aircraft Question. A trimmed aircraft has the following data. Is its longitudinal response stati- cally stable with the elevator held and with it free? ¯ V T = 0.8, h − h 0 = 0.1, dϵ dα = 0.4, a = 5.7 1/rad, a 1 = 5.0, a 2 = 4.0, a 3 = 0.5, b 1 = 0.05, b 2 = −0.10, b 3 = −0.05. All a i and b i have units 1/rad. Use the compact lift approximation introduced above. H n = (h 0 − h) + ¯ V T a 1 a (1 − dϵ dα ) = −0.1 + 0.8 · 5.0 5.7 · (1 − 0.4) = 0.321 , ¯a 1 = a 1 − a 2 b 1 b 2 = 5.0 − 4.0 · 0.05 −0.10 = 7.0 1/rad , H ′ n = (h 0 − h) + ¯ V T ¯a 1 a (1 − dϵ dα ) = −0.1 + 0.8 · 7.0 5.7 · (1 − 0.4) = 0.489 . Both margins are positive. This coefficient set gives a larger free margin because b 1 > 0. For a small increase ∆C L = 0.10: ∆C M,fixed ≃ −0.032 and ∆C M,free ≃ −0.049. Both moments are nose-down. a 3 and b 3 affect the trim settings. They do not enter these slopes because the tab is fixed during the disturbance. 7"},{"source":"lecture5a","page":8,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 5 Part 1 3 / FROM STABILITY TO TRIM The fixed margin sets the elevator trim gradient Now compare a sequence of trimmed conditions: C M = 0 at every point. Hold the tab and configuration fixed, but adjust the elevator as the required C L changes. 0 = C M 0 + (h − h 0 )C L − ¯ V T a 1 C L a 1 − dϵ dα + a 1 η T + a 2 η trim + a 3 β . Collect the elevator term and the terms multiplying C L : ¯ V T a 2 η trim = C M 0 − ¯ V T (a 1 η T + a 3 β) + (h − h 0 ) − ¯ V T a 1 a (1 − dϵ dα ) C L . The bracket is −H n . Therefore η trim = C M 0 ¯ V T a 2 − a 1 η T a 2 − a 3 β a 2 | {z } η 0 − H n ¯ V T a 2 C L . dη trim dC L = − H n ¯ V T a 2 . For H n > 0, ¯ V T > 0 and a 2 > 0, a larger required lift coefficient needs a more negative elevator angle: more up elevator. This gradient joins equilibria. The stability slope on the stick-fixed derivation described a disturbance with the elevator held; it did not set C M = 0 again after each change. 8"},{"source":"lecture5a","page":9,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 5 Part 1 3 / HINGE-TORQUE TRIM GRADIENT Substitute the trimmed elevator into the hinge law Along this curve the aircraft is trimmed, while the elevator is held at the required angle. Its hinge torque need not be zero. Insert the complete elevator trim equation from page 8 into the hinge law: C H,trim = b 1 C L a 1 − dϵ dα + η T + b 3 β + b 2 C M 0 ¯ V T a 2 − a 1 η T a 2 − a 3 β a 2 − C L H n ¯ V T a 2 . Collect the setting and tab terms: ¯ b 1 = b 1 − b 2 a 1 a 2 , ¯ b 3 = b 3 − b 2 a 3 a 2 . C H,trim = b 2 C M 0 ¯ V T a 2 + ¯ b 1 η T + ¯ b 3 β + b 1 a 1 − dϵ dα − b 2 H n ¯ V T a 2 C L . Factor b 2 /( ¯ V T a 2 ) from the coefficient of C L : b 1 a 1 − dϵ dα − b 2 H n ¯ V T a 2 = b 2 ¯ V T a 2 (h − h 0 ) − ¯ V T ¯a 1 a 1 − dϵ dα = − b 2 H ′ n ¯ V T a 2 . C H0 = b 2 C M 0 ¯ V T a 2 + ¯ b 1 η T + ¯ b 3 β. C H,trim = C H0 − b 2 C L H ′ n ¯ V T a 2 , dC H,trim dC L = − b 2 H ′ n ¯ V T a 2 . The free margin appears in the holding-torque gradient. This does not impose C H = 0 along the whole trim curve. 9"},{"source":"lecture5a","page":10,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 5 Part 1 3 / READING THE GRADIENTS Two trim gradients reveal two different margins H n dη trim /dC L positive fixed margin negative elevator slope H ′ n dC H,trim /dC L positive free margin positive hinge-coefficient slope Each point represents a different stability margin, with the other factors held fixed. These are plots of a gradient against margin, not elevator angle or torque against time. Elevator trim gradient Hinge-coefficient trim gradient dη trim dC L = − H n ¯ V T a 2 dC H,trim dC L = − b 2 H ′ n ¯ V T a 2 Zero when H n = 0. Zero when H ′ n = 0. Units: radians per unit C L . Dimensionless coefficient per unit C L . For an aft tail with a 2 > 0 and b 2 < 0, moving the CG aft reduces both margins. The elevator gradient becomes less negative; the hinge-coefficient gradient becomes less positive. A zero crossing identifies the neutral condition for that particular constraint. An aircraft can lose one positive margin while retaining the other. 10"},{"source":"lecture5a","page":11,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 5 Part 1 4 / AIRCRAFT CONTROL Stick, pedals and throttle have distinct roles pitch / roll input rudder pedals throttle Pilot input Conventional surface action Main initial effect Push forwards Elevator trailing edge down Nose-down pitching moment Pull backwards Elevator trailing edge up Nose-up pitching moment Stick / wheel right Right aileron up; left aileron down Right wing down Right pedal Rudder trailing edge right Nose yaws right Throttle forwards Increase engine thrust or power command Change energy input For the conventional aft tail, lowering the elevator increases tail lift. The upward force behind the CG produces a nose-down moment. Part 2 will relate the stick positions needed at different steady speeds to the trim gradients derived here. Throttle does not control speed independently of everything else. Speed, climb rate, drag and pitch trim interact. Control systems can also modify the direct response shown here. 11"},{"source":"lecture5a","page":12,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 5 Part 1 Conclusions Static longitudinal stability requires a restoring pitching-moment increment after a small incidence disturbance. For a positive lift slope: dC M dC L < 0. H n = (h 0 − h) + ¯ V T a 1 a 1 − dϵ dα , H ′ n = (h 0 − h) + ¯ V T ¯a 1 a 1 − dϵ dα , ¯a 1 = a 1 − a 2 b 1 b 2 . Positive margins are restoring. Zero margin is neutral. Moving the CG aft reduces both margins at fixed geometry. dη trim dC L = − H n ¯ V T a 2 , dC H,trim dC L = − b 2 H ′ n ¯ V T a 2 . For an aft tail with a 2 > 0 and b 2 < 0, positive margins give a negative elevator trim gradient and a positive hinge-coefficient trim gradient. Part 2 uses these two gradients to find how the stick position and pilot force change with steady flight speed. Tutorial 3, questions 4–6, apply the margin and tab-trim relationships. 12"},{"source":"lecture5b","page":1,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 5 Part 2 PART 2 / STICK GEARING AND FORCE From elevator angle to stick movement and force Part 1 supplied the elevator and hinge-coefficient trim gradients. Start by connecting the elevator to the pilot’s stick. Take s positive forwards and η positive for elevator trailing edge down. With suitable reference origins: η = G η s, dη = G η ds, ds dη = 1 G η . G η is the stick gearing in rad/m; G η > 0. +s, forwards stick G η +η elevator down fixed tail nose Let P η be the pilot force, positive for a push. The control linkage supplies holding torque τ hold = −H, opposing the aerodynamic hinge torque H. Equate the work transmitted through an ideal reversible linkage: P η ds = τ hold dη = τ hold G η ds. P η = G η τ hold = −G η H. The work is in joules, torque in newton metres and force in newtons. In magnitude, |P η | = G η |H|. This lecture uses the ideal linkage model, without friction or artificial feel. 1"},{"source":"lecture5b","page":2,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 5 Part 2 1 / LIFT COEFFICIENT AND SPEED Differentiate the steady level-flight balance Compare trimmed level-flight conditions at the same weight, density and configuration: L = W = 1 2 ρU 2 ∞ S W C L . U ∞ is the air-relative flight speed, written V in Week 4. Rearrange before differentiat- ing: U 2 ∞ C L = 2W ρS W = constant. Apply the product rule: d(U 2 ∞ C L ) = 0, 2U ∞ C L dU ∞ + U 2 ∞ dC L = 0, U 2 ∞ dC L = −2U ∞ C L dU ∞ . Divide by U 2 ∞ dU ∞ : dC L dU ∞ = − 2C L U ∞ . Higher speed gives more dynamic pressure. The same weight can therefore be supported with a smaller lift coefficient. This slope has units s/m. Weight and density remain constant during this differentiation. 2"},{"source":"lecture5b","page":3,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 5 Part 2 2 / STICK MOVEMENT AND SPEED Apply the chain rule to the stick position Speed changes the required C L . That changes the trimmed elevator angle, which changes the stick position: U ∞ −→ C L −→ η trim −→ s. ds dU ∞ = ds dη dη trim dC L dC L dU ∞ . Insert each factor separately: ds dη = 1 G η , dη trim dC L = − H n ¯ V T a 2 , dC L dU ∞ = − 2C L U ∞ . ds dU ∞ = 1 G η − H n ¯ V T a 2   − 2C L U ∞ = 2C L G η U ∞ ¯ V T a 2 H n . At the reference equilibrium, denoted by subscript e: ds dU ∞ e = 2C L,e G η U ∞,e ¯ V T a 2 H n . For an aft tail with positive fixed margin, the slope is positive: higher steady speed needs a more forward stick position. The derivative describes a sequence of equilibria, with the tab fixed. 3"},{"source":"lecture5b","page":4,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 5 Part 2 3 / HINGE COEFFICIENT AND SPEED Apply the chain rule to the hinge coefficient Use the second trim gradient from Part 1: dC H,trim dC L = − b 2 H ′ n ¯ V T a 2 . dC H,trim dU ∞ = dC H,trim dC L dC L dU ∞ = − b 2 H ′ n ¯ V T a 2   − 2C L U ∞ = 2C L b 2 H ′ n U ∞ ¯ V T a 2 . H ′ n > 0, b 2 < 0, ¯ V T , a 2 > 0 =⇒ dC H,trim dU ∞ < 0. Convert the coefficient into aerodynamic hinge torque and pilot holding force: H = qS e ¯c e C H , P η = −G η qS e ¯c e C H . S e and ¯c e are the elevator reference area and chord. At a force-free trim speed, C H = 0; a small speed increase then requires a forward holding force for H ′ n > 0. The moment and gearing method also applies to a canard when its signed arm and local-flow relation are used. The sign conclusions above assume a conventional aft tail. 4"},{"source":"lecture5b","page":5,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 5 Part 2 4 / EXAMPLE 1 Calculate the two trim gradients Question. Find dη trim /dC L and dC H,trim /dC L for the following aircraft: H n = 0.10, H ′ n = 0.05, ¯ V T = 0.50, a 2 = 1.0 rad −1 , b 2 = −0.04 rad −1 . First use the fixed margin: dη trim dC L = − H n ¯ V T a 2 = − 0.10 0.50 · 1.0 = −0.20 rad . Then use the free margin: dC H,trim dC L = − b 2 H ′ n ¯ V T a 2 = − (−0.04) · 0.05 0.50 · 1.0 = 4.0 × 10 −3 . A larger required C L needs more up-elevator and a larger aerodynamic hinge coefficient for these supplied signs. These are gradients per unit C L . The next example converts them into gradients with respect to speed. 5"},{"source":"lecture5b","page":6,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 5 Part 2 4 / EXAMPLE 2 Calculate stick movement and coefficient change Question. For the same aircraft, use dC L dU ∞ = −0.02 s/m, G η = 1.0 rad/m. Find ds/dU ∞ and dC H,trim /dU ∞ . ds dU ∞ = 1 G η dη trim dC L dC L dU ∞ = 1 1.0 · (−0.20) · (−0.02) = 4.0 × 10 −3 s . dC H,trim dU ∞ = dC H,trim dC L dC L dU ∞ = (4.0 × 10 −3 ) · (−0.02) = −8.0 × 10 −5 s/m . A small 1 m/s increase in steady speed requires about 4 mm of forward stick move- ment. The unit of ds/dU ∞ is m/(m/s) = s. It is displacement per speed change, rather than a response time. 6"},{"source":"lecture5b","page":7,"text":"EMS612U Flight dynamics and stability of aircraft Week 5 Part 2 Conclusions Gearing connects the elevator angle to the stick displacement: dη = G η ds, P η = G η τ hold = −G η H. At fixed weight and density, steady level flight gives dC L dU ∞ = − 2C L U ∞ . The two chains then give ds dU ∞ = 2C L H n G η U ∞ ¯ V T a 2 , dC H,trim dU ∞ = 2C L b 2 H ′ n U ∞ ¯ V T a 2 . For the stated aft-tail signs, a positive fixed margin gives forward stick movement as steady speed increases. A positive free margin gives a decreasing hinge coefficient and, near force-free trim, increasing forward holding force. Part 1 connects CG position to stability and trim gradients. Part 2 connects those gradients to the pilot’s controls. Week 6 extends the stability reasoning to sideslip, roll and yaw. 7"}];
